data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR gene editing is a genetic engineering technique based on a simplified version of the bacterial CRISPR-Cas9 antiviral defense system [1].\n- Cas9 nuclease, delivered into a cell with a synthetic guide RNA (gRNA), can cut the genome at a desired location [1].\n- Genome editing inserts, deletes, modifies, or replaces DNA at site-specific locations, using molecular scissors to make double-strand breaks [3].\n- In bacteria and archaea, CRISPR sequences come from past bacteriophage DNA and are used to detect and destroy similar phage DNA in later infections [2].\n- Headlines link CRISPR to treating a broad array of diseases [4] and to eliminating HIV in cell culture [5]."}

