data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"analyst","content":"- **Core mechanism:** Cas9 nuclease, guided by a synthetic gRNA, cuts DNA at a chosen site [1]. The resulting double-strand breaks let researchers insert, delete, modify, or replace DNA at specific locations [3].\n\n- **Natural origin:** CRISPR is adapted from a bacterial and archaeal antiviral system. It stores fragments of past phage DNA and uses them to detect and destroy matching phage DNA in later infections [2][1].\n\n- **Hype versus scope:** Headlines link CRISPR to treating many diseases [4] and to eliminating HIV, but the HIV claim comes from cell culture work [5]. Treat broad clinical promises cautiously until they reach patients."}

